From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_BANK_HASH_SKIP=1: the once-per-frame upload of a native 64 KiB uniform bank is skipped while its bytes match its last upload and nothing else wrote its host range (Lichii #1131); off by default

Port of zortos293/Lichii PR #1131 by liomaroliveira ("buffer_cache: skip re-uploading native
uniform banks whose bytes did not change", commit 1d8e4e2db6, open on 10 Nov). Upstream measured
Ocarina of Time in Kokiri Forest on an RX 6700 XT with RADV: ~212 banks a frame, 83% unchanged and
skipped, 42.3 -> 42.7/43.1 fps over two runs (within noise). That number does not carry over to
the console.

What it does. Native programs read their 64 KiB banks with indirect offsets, and writes to that
memory can be missed by the memory tracker, so each bank is uploaded again once a frame (0039;
EDEN_NX2_BANK_REUPLOAD unset or 1). With EDEN_UP_BANK_HASH_SKIP=1 (Common::PeSwitch, exactly "1")
that upload first hashes the bank's guest bytes, page by page (CityHash64 chained per page;
upstream uses XXH3, which this tree does not carry). When the hash, size and buffer equal the
bank's entry, the host buffer holds those bytes already and the upload is skipped. A write the
tracker missed changes the hash, so the bank is still uploaded. Unset: nothing is hashed, no entry
is kept, every path is as before.

This keeps the re-upload; it only skips identical bytes. The ledger's EDEN_NX2_BANK_REUPLOAD=4 and
EDEN_NX2_UBO_BANK_DIRECT (flash tests, off) are different switches and are left as they are. Modes
0, 2, 3 and 4 are untouched; only mode 1 (the default) skips.

Changes from upstream:

1. Our mode 1 marks the bank CPU-modified and SynchronizeBuffer uploads it (upstream copies it
   directly, which is our EDEN_NX2_UBO_BANK_DIRECT=1). Both are covered. On the mark path the
   entry is kept only after SynchronizeBuffer in the same bind uploaded something (it then took
   every marked page). A bank this bind streams from guest memory (at most 4 KiB with the skip
   cache, or misaligned) is marked as before and not hashed: nothing reaches the host buffer
   there, so an entry could not be kept.

2. Upstream keeps an entry until the bank's bytes change or its buffer is deleted. That misses one
   case: after the entry, the host range changed (a tracked write was uploaded, or a GPU, DMA or
   inline write), and a write the tracker missed then put the entry's bytes back. The hash
   matches, the host holds the newer bytes, the bank is skipped and shows stale uniforms.
   Per-draw material data that the game rewrites between draws comes back to the same
   frame-start bytes every frame in a still scene, which is the white-material flash Eglantine
   031e07436c chased. Here every write into a host buffer drops the entries it overlaps
   (Nx2BankHashForget): UploadMemory (every tracker upload and both bank paths),
   InlineMemoryImplementation (inline memory and inline page writes), DMACopy and DMAClear
   destinations, MarkWrittenBuffer (shader, transform feedback, image-to-buffer and query writes
   the cache marks), DiscardWrite, inline index uploads, the query cache's value copies
   (SyncValues, which the cache does not record) and DeleteBuffer (slot ids are reused, as
   upstream). An entry stands only while nothing but the bank's own upload wrote its range.
   Unlike EDEN_UP_L949_UBO_KEEP (UNSAFE in the ledger), nothing here trusts guest write
   notifications: the hash reads guest memory itself at every check. The hooks are for host
   buffer writes, which only the buffer cache makes.

3. EDEN_UP_AHEAD_GUARD=2 or 3 (0491-0504): an upload reads a page that has a copy from the copy
   (the bytes the frame in flight was presented with), while the hash reads guest memory. An
   entry kept then would name bytes the host buffer does not hold; after the fence the bank would
   be skipped and show the older bytes until the redirty gather. So a bank with a copied page
   (AheadGuard::HasCopy, lock-free) is uploaded as before and its entry dropped, and HasCopy is
   checked again after the upload, before an entry is kept. With no copy in the bank the old
   upload also reads guest memory, so a skip gives the frame in flight the same bytes. Held
   writes (=3) reach guest memory while a copy stands, so they meet the HasCopy rule; after the
   guard is lifted they differ from the entry's bytes and the bank is uploaded. Needs 0496
   (HasCopy, CopyMode).

4. Before an entry is kept the bank is hashed again. A guest write that landed while it was
   uploaded (the upload may hold the bytes from before or after it) leaves no entry. Upstream
   keeps the first hash, so a missed write in that window, later undone by another, was skipped.

5. At most 16384 entries (all dropped when reached; an entry less only costs an upload), and a
   bitmap of 1 MiB regions (8 KiB, made at the first entry) so a write elsewhere skips the map.

6. Log, with the switch only, every 600 frames: "EDEN_UP_BANK_HASH_SKIP: native banks in 600
   frames: U uploaded (K KiB), S unchanged skipped, F entries dropped by another write, C uploads
   left to the ahead guard (a copied page), M written while uploaded, T streamed (marked,
   unhashed); E entries".

Left as it is:
- A guest write and its exact undo, both inside the few microseconds between the first hash and
  the second, both missed by the tracker.
- A GPU write the cache does not mark (a shader writing a buffer bound read-only) is no longer
  overwritten by the once-per-frame upload.
- A 64-bit hash collision.

Runs on the PS5: same core path. Pool memory: up to 16384 map nodes (~1.5 MiB) and 8 KiB, only
with the switch. Cost with it: one CityHash64 over each hashed bank once a frame (upstream averaged
3.5 KiB a bank, so about 0.75 MiB a frame at 212 banks) and a second one over each bank uploaded,
a map lookup per bank, and a bitmap test on each host buffer write (a map walk only when it
hits). Saved: for each skipped bank the mark
(page protection flips), the staging copy and the transfer.
v2 (review): a skipped bank still notes its pages for EDEN_UP_AHEAD_GUARD (OverwriteNoteRead,
as the mark path's upload did in SynchronizeBufferPages). Without it, three skipped frames took
the bank out of the guard's guess, so a later frame's write to it was neither copied nor waited
for, and the next upload gave the frame in flight the later bytes. And the copy check is made
again after the hash: a page copied while it was hashed is uploaded as before, no skip.

Hang: no new wait. Not compiled, not run.

